      "content": "Ah, the DNS propagation, a tale as old as the internet itself, but with a twist of our little mischievous goblin friends. You see, when you change the A records, it's like sending out a bunch of goblins to spread the word about a new treasure map. Some goblins are quick and eager, darting off to the nearest node, while others are slow, perhaps distracted by a shiny trinket or a particularly tasty snack.\n\nIn the realm of DNS, the TTL, or Time to Live, is like the goblins' sense of urgency. It tells them how long to hold onto the old map before they check for updates. However, not all nodes are goblins of the same breed. Some are more diligent, checking for updates more frequently, while others are a bit more laid-back, sticking to the old map for longer.\n\nThe inconsistency you're seeing is like a goblin party where some goblins have received the new map while others are still holding onto the old one. It's a bit of a mess, isn't it? But fear not, for this is all part of the grand adventure of the internet. Just remember, in the world of DNS, patience is a virtue, and sometimes you just",
