      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Apologies for the confusion. Let's dive into the technical aspects of DNS propagation. When you update the A records for a domain, the new IP address is distributed across the DNS servers around the world. This process is known as DNS propagation.\n\nThe Time to Live (TTL) value in the DNS records is a setting that determines how long a DNS server should cache the record before checking for updates. When you change the A records, the new TTL value will dictate how long it takes for the changes to propagate to all DNS servers.\n\nHowever, not all DNS servers update their records at the same time. Some servers may check for updates more frequently than others, or they may be located in different geographical regions, leading to variations in propagation times. Additionally, some DNS servers may have caching mechanisms that retain the old records for longer periods, even after the TTL has expired.\n\nIn some cases, DNS servers may also be using different DNS resolvers, which can lead to inconsistencies in propagation. The resolvers themselves may have different caching policies and update frequencies, which can result in some nodes seeing the old IP while others see the new one.\n\nTo ensure more consistent propagation, you can try reducing the TTL value before making the changes, so that DNS servers update",
